WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
OpenAI is shipping purpose-trained frontier models to defenders first, giving authorized teams GPT 5.6 cyber for vulnerability research before attackers can weaponize similar capabilities. This is Models &amp; Agents Episode 138. OpenAI expanded its daybreak program with two new access tiers called daybreak blue for broad defensive work and daybreak red for advanced authorized testing. Both tiers send

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:59.940
enter on GPT 5.6 variants that have already surfaced real zero days in Chrome's V8 engine. Builders working in security tooling or red team automation should watch how the safeguards and access controls evolve this week. OpenAI announced the expansion of its daybreak cyber security initiative alongside GPT 5.6 cyber, a purpose-trained model for authorized vulnerability research, exploit validation and secure

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:24.360
security testing. Daybreak Blue offers frontier models including GPT 5.6 sole with safeguards tuned for vulnerability discovery, secure code review, malware analysis, incident response, and patch validation. Daybreak Red adds the specialized GPT 5.6 cyber variant aimed at experienced defenders handling complex challenges.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:39.540
The company states the models have uncovered previously unknown vulnerabilities in open source projects such as Chrome's V8 engine. Sam Ultman separately encouraged users to consider the models for defending their own systems.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:48.060
This development builds directly on yesterday's frontier model coverage by shifting emphasis from general capability to defensive deployment.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:02:08.800
What to watch is how access controls and red teaming requirements tighten as more teams request the specialized weights. Meta released Muse Glimmer 30B, its first Apache 2.0 licensed open weight model. Earlier Lama releases used a non-OSI license that restricted commercial use.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:22.540
The 30B checkpoint reportedly fits on a single RTX 3090. Simon Willison published initial notes on the models licensing shift and practical implications for open weight users.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:41.600
Layton Space Coverage highlighted the release as a small win for American open models. Web AI released Twire LM, a pair of formal logic models at 1.7B and 3B parameters designed for English to first order logic translation and premise conclusion checking.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:03:00.920
The 3B variant runs on CPU or 4GB VRAM while the 1.7B download is 1.06GB. Both carry a non-commercial license. The model card notes that headline benchmark scores come from an unreleased checkpoint rather than the published weights.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:14.920
An unreleased research version of Claude raised the verified lower bound on the fraction of Raymond Zeta 0's satisfying the hypothesis from 40 to 1.6% to 67.2%.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:25.940
The model did not solve the full Raymond hypothesis. The result demonstrates incremental progress on a long-standing number theory problem through targeted prompting.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:36.080
A new diffusion language model called Steerling 8B was trained with interpretability constraints built into the objective rather than applied post hoc.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:45.940
The model attributes generated tokens to input tokens, human understandable concepts and training data, enabling closed loop steering without retraining.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:04:04.240
It remains competitive with open models trained on 2 to 16 times more compute. Doc Atlas treats long document understanding as a mutable state information seeking process with an external harness that exposes search, reading, note taking and review tools.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:22.240
The system maintains a hierarchical tree and note store while updating both as the agent records evidence. With GPT 5.4, it reaches 71.4% on MML long bench dashed doc above the human expert baseline of 65.8%.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:35.340
The Q1 3.5 to 4B VLM trained end to end inside the harness scores 63.7% versus a 54.4% direct input baseline.

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:45.380
Search G1 supplies representation based intrinsic rewards that estimate whether retrieval is necessary and whether the final answer is evident sensitive.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:53.080
The method periodically refits readouts on the latest policy trajectories, so rewards co-evolve with the model.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:05:05.060
Experiments across search-based QA benchmarks show shorter response trajectories at competitive accuracy without requiring process annotations or LLM judges during training.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:15.360
A complete programmable pipeline for Mini Max H3 multimodal video and audio generation is demonstrated using comfy UI as a headless back end.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:23.500
The guide covers hardware profiling, model weight downloading, dynamic graph construction and joint video audio decoding.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:32.280
Developers already running comfy UI workflows can integrate the pipeline for automated multimodal inference without custom server code.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:42.820
A new evaluation suite pairs the UniHall fine grain data set with self-adaptive multimodal fuzzing that evolves inputs to expose hallucination boundaries.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:53.140
State of the art MLLM's show significant degradation under fuzzing compared with static benchmarks, revealing a helpfulness hallucination trade-off.

00:05:53.140 --> 00:06:11.880
The full framework, code and benchmark are released on GitHub. Survey review supplies 675 annotated survey papers and 1,630 review reports converted into four-dimensional scores for readability, criticalness, comprehensiveness and structure.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:23.360
Alora tuned Q3-32B baseline called survey align reduces average MSE from 2.28 to 1.38 against human reviewers.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:41.860
The data set and evaluation protocol are public with standardized train test splits. POVISE LE provides 1,117 images and 2,366 manually annotated VQA pairs focused on culturally grounded Polish VQA.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:51.000
The data set targets failures in region-specific symbolic content and pragmatic cues that English-centric training misses.

00:06:51.720 --> 00:07:00.960
Test Muse Glimmer 30B locally if you need an Apache 2.0 model that fits on 1.3090 for open-weight experimentation.

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:10.100
Run the doc Atlas harness on MM long bench hyphen doc documents to see whether mutable state improves your current agent's evidence tracking.

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:18.860
Add the SAMF fuzzing suite to your multimodal evaluation pipeline to surface hallucination modes that static benchmarks miss.

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:27.600
Compare survey align against your current LLM as judge setup on the released survey review data set for better human alignment.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:37.320
Try the 12LM3B checkpoint on CPU hardware for English to logic translation tasks where formal verification is required.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:44.580
More teams are expected to request daybreak red access as real-world vulnerability results accumulate.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:51.860
Additional open-weight formal logic models may appear following the twice LM non-commercial release pattern.

00:07:52.440 --> 00:08:01.080
Culturally specific VQA benchmarks like POVISE LE are likely to expand to other languages with similar annotation depth.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:05.900
Okay, let's pop the hood on mutable state document harnesses.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:11.100
Everyone treats long document agents as simple retrieval plus generation pipelines.

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:23.940
In practice, doc Atlas shows the engineering reality is a live external environment that decides that each step what to search, read, store, review or surface under a fixed context budget.

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:28.640
The harness maintains a hierarchical tree plus a separate note store.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:33.260
Both structures are mutated by the agent rather than rebuilt from scratch.

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:42.240
This design lets the same tooling support both inference time use with large VLMs and end-to-end RL for compact agents.

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:55.280
The performance jump from 54.4% to 63.7% on a 4B model comes from selective evidence access and active working memory rather than larger context windows.

00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:04.460
The approach also combines self-improving retrieval with review tools so the agent can revisit earlier decisions without exceeding token limits.

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:17.540
When the document spans dozens of pages with tables, figures, and cross references, the mutable harness avoids the common failure mode of static indexes that lock in an initial evidence set.

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:26.020
The practical decision is straightforward. Adopt the mutable harness when your workload involves repeated evidence gathering across many pages.

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:34.160
Stay with static rag when the document fits comfortably in one forward pass and latency matters more than iterative refinement.

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:45.660
Before we go, teams evaluating open-weight options will want to see whether Muse glimmers Apache 2.0 license triggers follow-on releases from other labs this month.

